"""Adaptive budget (rl/gen7/build_adaptive.py: 0.55 soft, 0.75 max, ext 1, change 0) on g42r. Ablation variant of omni (rl/gen6/variant.py): USE_REVISIT=1

Gated lock guard (rl/gen7/build_guard2.py: still>=4, home<=2). Wrongway Arena agent "omni2" (gen7): omni with the USE_BOOK layer split into USE_BOOK (entries),
USE_LOCK_GUARD, USE_REVISIT, USE_LOST_SEARCH and GUARD_OWN_CLOCK (rl/gen7/build_omni2.py).

Wrongway Arena agent "omni" (gen6): every component of the 7 Nov 2026 research in one file.

Single file, standard library only, Python 3.9 compatible.  Built by rl/gen6/build_omni.py
from the gen5 agents; every component sits behind a module-level switch so any one of them
can be turned off for an ablation (rl/gen6/variant.py writes the ablation files; agents are
immutable, so a variant is a new file, never an edit).

  base               agents/gen5/fastsearch_ng.py: deepsearch's alpha-beta with the ten exact
                     speed-ups of rl/speed (x1.16 nodes/s, node-identical choices) and the
                     near-goal frontier fix (blocking walls are kept when a pawn is one step out).
  USE_BOOK           agents/gen5/bookfast5.py layer: opening book book5 (14 entries, both seats,
                     mirror-aware, validated against the legal lists, any miss -> search), the
                     lock guard (a wall when the opponent is <= LOCK_TRIG walls from locking us
                     behind its own pawn), the anti-shuffle REVISIT_TAX at the root, and the
                     root rule that keeps searching in lost positions so the tax can act.  The
                     strict clock (cyclic gc paused while thinking, the search closure cycle cut
                     after every move) is always on: it cannot change a choice.
  USE_TEXEL          agents/gen5/texel_ng.py evaluation: a wall in hand is worth
                     WALL_BY_DIST[ds + dt] instead of a flat 14, TEMPO 4 (results/texel.md).
  USE_POLICY         agents/gen5/policy_ng.py: int8 policy model (tables + 64-hidden MLP,
                     stdlib inference) orders the root and interior candidates and gives extra
                     late-move reductions to candidates far below the best (results/policy.md).
  USE_RACE_SOLVER    agents/gen5/endgame_ng.py: exact alpha-beta over the two pawns when a side
                     has no walls; one TT per wall set, node- and deadline-limited, falls back to
                     the distance formula when over budget (results/endgame.md).  DEFAULT OFF: at
                     every node below the last wall it costs up to 300 solver nodes, x0.34 nodes/s
                     over the benchmark and -1.7 ply at the clock (results/gen6.md); the A/B of
                     omni_race1 vs omni decides whether the exact race knowledge pays for that.
  WALL_FRONTIER_BONUS agents/gen5/horizon_ng.py: credit to a wall scored statically at a frontier
                     node (results/horizon.md, weak evidence; default 0 = off).

With every switch off the engine is node-identical to fastsearch_ng; with exactly one switch on
it is node-identical to that component's gen5 agent (rl/speed/bench.py check, results/gen6.md).

Design of the shared engine (deepsearch):
  * Distance fields: each wall set has one 81-entry distance-to-goal field
    per player, cached under a Zobrist hash of the walls.  Placing a wall
    repairs the parent's field incrementally (only cells that lost every
    shortest-path parent are relabelled); most walls change nothing and the
    parent's list is simply shared.
  * Wall gains (how far a wall pushes a pawn back) come from an 81-bit
    bitboard flood fill that stops at the first untouched "ring" of the old
    field, with a cache, and with a per-search table of walls that already
    disconnect the board at the root.  No make/unmake is needed for them.
  * Search: iterative deepening, aspiration windows, principal-variation
    search, transposition table, killer + history ordering, late-move
    reductions, repetition detection inside a line (shuffling scores 0),
    and frontier nodes (depth 1) that score all their children inline.
  * Wall generation: only walls crossing the opponent's current shortest
    path; near the leaves only those that really lengthen it; at the root
    also a few defensive walls that remove the opponent's best cut.
  * Evaluation: path-length difference, walls in hand, tempo, plus race
    rules once the side that could still interfere has no walls left.
"""
